1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,856
Walking barefoot in a Chitwan forest, you step on a thorn.

2
00:00:03,856 --> 00:00:07,957
Before you even feel the pain, your foot has already jumped back.

3
00:00:07,957 --> 00:00:08,934
How fast was that?

4
00:00:09,167 --> 00:00:11,564
About twenty one thousandths of a second.

5
00:00:11,564 --> 00:00:19,794
Now take away one fatty wrapping around the nerve fibres, and the same reflex would take three and a half seconds.

6
00:00:19,794 --> 00:00:21,929
Long enough for the thorn to go deep.

7
00:00:21,929 --> 00:00:28,194
That wrapping is a tissue, and by the end of this lesson you will be able to calculate both numbers yourself.

8
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:41,215
Welcome to Class 12 Biology, Unit 6: Animal Tissues, the first unit of zoology.

9
00:00:41,215 --> 00:00:44,978
The whole animal body is built from just four tissues.

10
00:00:44,978 --> 00:00:49,500
Learn them properly now, and every organ that follows becomes easy.

11
00:00:51,900 --> 00:00:53,803
Four tissues, four parts.

12
00:00:53,803 --> 00:00:57,631
Epithelial tissue, the coverings and linings.

13
00:00:57,631 --> 00:01:00,925
Connective tissue, from fat to bone to blood.

14
00:01:00,925 --> 00:01:04,623
Muscular tissue, and how a sarcomere contracts.

15
00:01:04,623 --> 00:01:09,357
And nervous tissue: the neuron, the myelin sheath, and the synapse.

16
00:01:13,700 --> 00:01:22,790
Epithelium is a sheet of tightly packed cells, with almost nothing between them, resting on a thin non cellular basement membrane.

17
00:01:22,790 --> 00:01:27,085
It covers the body's surfaces and lines its cavities and tubes.

18
00:01:27,300 --> 00:01:30,453
Its characters follow from its job as a boundary.

19
00:01:30,453 --> 00:01:34,906
The cells are joined by junctions, so the sheet is leak proof.

20
00:01:34,906 --> 00:01:38,122
It has a free surface and a basal surface.

21
00:01:38,122 --> 00:01:44,372
It has no blood vessels at all: food and oxygen diffuse up from the capillaries beneath.

22
00:01:44,372 --> 00:01:48,695
It regrows fast, because it is the layer that wears away.

23
00:01:48,695 --> 00:01:53,151
And it is the only tissue that arises from all three germ layers.

24
00:01:55,367 --> 00:01:56,788
Simple squamous.

25
00:01:56,788 --> 00:02:01,710
Flat, tile like cells, thin enough for gases to diffuse across.

26
00:02:01,710 --> 00:02:07,214
Lungs' alveoli, Bowman's capsule in the kidney, and the lining of every blood vessel.

27
00:02:07,433 --> 00:02:08,711
Simple cuboidal.

28
00:02:08,711 --> 00:02:12,148
Cube shaped cells with a round central nucleus.

29
00:02:12,148 --> 00:02:18,241
Kidney tubules, thyroid follicles and the ducts of glands, for absorbing and secreting.

30
00:02:18,467 --> 00:02:19,745
Simple columnar.

31
00:02:19,745 --> 00:02:28,456
Tall cells with the nucleus near the base, lining the stomach and the small intestine, where a lot of secreting and absorbing happens.

32
00:02:28,667 --> 00:02:39,827
Give columnar cells cilia, and the beating hairs sweep things along: mucus in the bronchioles, the egg along the oviduct, cerebrospinal fluid in the brain's ventricles.

33
00:02:39,827 --> 00:02:42,038
That is ciliated columnar epithelium.

34
00:02:43,267 --> 00:02:46,823
Pseudostratified epithelium is a trick of the eye.

35
00:02:46,823 --> 00:02:53,464
The cells are of different heights, so their nuclei sit at two levels and the sheet looks layered.

36
00:02:53,464 --> 00:02:57,006
But every single cell stands on the basement membrane.

37
00:02:57,006 --> 00:02:58,620
It is one layer thick.

38
00:02:58,620 --> 00:03:00,628
It lines the trachea and the nose.

39
00:03:02,233 --> 00:03:06,961
Where wear is heavy, the epithelium is many cells thick.

40
00:03:06,961 --> 00:03:13,029
Only the deepest layer, the germinative layer, sits on the basement membrane and divides.

41
00:03:13,029 --> 00:03:17,344
Its daughters are pushed upward, flatten, die and are shed from the top.

42
00:03:17,567 --> 00:03:25,798
In the skin, the dying cells fill with the tough protein keratin, giving a dry, waterproof keratinised surface.

43
00:03:25,798 --> 00:03:31,871
In the mouth, oesophagus and vagina the surface stays moist and is non keratinised.

44
00:03:33,100 --> 00:03:37,932
Transitional epithelium lines the ureter and the urinary bladder.

45
00:03:37,932 --> 00:03:40,367
Its cells can slide over one another.

46
00:03:40,367 --> 00:03:46,591
When the bladder is empty the lining is four or five cells thick with domed surface cells.

47
00:03:46,591 --> 00:03:51,738
As it fills, it stretches to just two or three thin layers.

48
00:03:53,967 --> 00:03:55,362
Exam tip.

49
00:03:55,362 --> 00:03:58,265
Pseudostratified is not stratified.

50
00:03:58,265 --> 00:04:05,818
In pseudostratified epithelium every cell touches the basement membrane, so it is one layer.

51
00:04:05,818 --> 00:04:09,694
In a stratified epithelium only the deepest layer does.

52
00:04:10,467 --> 00:04:17,044
Epithelial cells that specialise for secretion sink into the tissue below and form glands.

53
00:04:17,044 --> 00:04:23,828
A single goblet cell is a unicellular gland; many cells together make a multicellular gland.

54
00:04:23,828 --> 00:04:31,185
Exocrine glands release through a duct; endocrine glands have no duct and release hormones into the blood.

55
00:04:31,185 --> 00:04:34,370
And there are three ways a cell can let go of its secretion.

56
00:04:34,600 --> 00:04:42,102
Merocrine: the secretion leaves by exocytosis and the cell stays whole, as in the salivary glands.

57
00:04:42,102 --> 00:04:47,974
Apocrine: the tip of the cell pinches off with the secretion, as in the mammary gland.

58
00:04:47,974 --> 00:04:54,455
Holocrine: the whole cell breaks down and becomes the secretion, as in the sebaceous glands of the skin.

59
00:04:55,667 --> 00:04:56,736
A quick calculation.

60
00:04:56,967 --> 00:05:01,187
Actual size is image size divided by magnification.

61
00:05:01,187 --> 00:05:08,336
Forty millimetres over a thousand is zero point zero four millimetres, which is forty micrometres.

62
00:05:08,336 --> 00:05:10,744
The width is eight micrometres.

63
00:05:10,967 --> 00:05:17,883
A millimetre is a thousand micrometres, so a thousand over eight: one hundred and twenty five cells.

64
00:05:17,883 --> 00:05:22,219
Forty tall and eight wide: five times taller than broad.

65
00:05:22,219 --> 00:05:23,486
That is what columnar means.

66
00:05:25,900 --> 00:05:27,100
Pause and think.

67
00:05:27,100 --> 00:05:29,509
Which organ needs a lining that can stretch?

68
00:05:36,400 --> 00:05:38,863
The urinary bladder, and the ureter.

69
00:05:38,863 --> 00:05:42,027
Its cells slide over each other as it fills.

70
00:05:42,027 --> 00:05:50,675
The trachea has pseudostratified ciliated epithelium, the intestine simple columnar, and the oesophagus stratified squamous.

71
00:05:55,033 --> 00:05:59,136
Connective tissue is the mirror image of epithelium.

72
00:05:59,136 --> 00:06:07,873
Here the cells are few and far apart, and the work is done by a large intercellular matrix of fibres and ground substance.

73
00:06:07,873 --> 00:06:11,543
It binds, supports, packs, transports and defends.

74
00:06:11,767 --> 00:06:14,972
Three kinds of fibre run through the matrix.

75
00:06:14,972 --> 00:06:23,644
White collagen fibres: thick, wavy bundles, very strong and inelastic; boil them and they become gelatin.

76
00:06:23,644 --> 00:06:31,287
Yellow elastic fibres: thin, branching, stretching to one and a half times their length and snapping back.

77
00:06:31,287 --> 00:06:36,045
And fine reticular fibres, the mesh of lymph nodes and the spleen.

78
00:06:36,267 --> 00:06:37,401
And the cells.

79
00:06:37,401 --> 00:06:41,750
Fibroblasts make the fibres and ground substance.

80
00:06:41,750 --> 00:06:45,709
Macrophages crawl about engulfing bacteria and debris.

81
00:06:45,709 --> 00:06:53,808
Mast cells release heparin, which stops blood clotting inside vessels, and histamine, which drives inflammation and allergy.

82
00:06:53,808 --> 00:06:57,844
Plasma cells make antibodies, and adipocytes store fat.

83
00:06:59,467 --> 00:07:05,120
Connective tissue comes in four groups: loose, dense, skeletal and fluid.

84
00:07:05,120 --> 00:07:11,591
Loose areolar tissue is the body's packing material, under the skin and around every organ.

85
00:07:11,591 --> 00:07:15,575
Fill it with fat cells and it becomes adipose tissue.

86
00:07:15,575 --> 00:07:23,614
Each fat cell is almost entirely one big droplet, with the nucleus squeezed to one side like a signet ring.

87
00:07:24,833 --> 00:07:27,100
Two dense tissues, always compared.

88
00:07:27,100 --> 00:07:31,293
A tendon is packed with parallel bundles of white collagen.

89
00:07:31,293 --> 00:07:33,988
Pull it, and it barely stretches.

90
00:07:33,988 --> 00:07:37,816
A ligament has branching yellow elastic fibres.

91
00:07:37,816 --> 00:07:40,611
Pull it, and it stretches, then springs back.

92
00:07:40,833 --> 00:07:46,811
The four marks: fibre, arrangement, elasticity, and what each connects.

93
00:07:46,811 --> 00:07:48,725
Tendon: muscle to bone.

94
00:07:48,725 --> 00:07:50,736
Ligament: bone to bone at a joint.

95
00:07:51,967 --> 00:07:54,560
Cartilage is firm but flexible.

96
00:07:54,560 --> 00:08:03,219
Its cells, chondrocytes, sit alone or in twos in little spaces called lacunae, in a matrix of the protein chondrin.

97
00:08:03,219 --> 00:08:09,973
It is wrapped in a perichondrium, and it has no blood vessels at all: it is fed only by slow diffusion.

98
00:08:11,200 --> 00:08:13,793
Bone is the hardest connective tissue.

99
00:08:13,793 --> 00:08:22,907
Its matrix is about one third protein, for toughness, and two thirds calcium salts, mainly calcium phosphate, for hardness.

100
00:08:22,907 --> 00:08:28,116
In mammalian compact bone it is built in cylinders called Haversian systems.

101
00:08:28,116 --> 00:08:32,145
A central Haversian canal carries a blood vessel and a nerve.

102
00:08:32,367 --> 00:08:37,369
Around it, four to twenty concentric rings of matrix, the lamellae.

103
00:08:37,369 --> 00:08:46,665
Between them, the osteocytes in their lacunae, each linked to its neighbours and to the canal by hair fine channels, the canaliculi.

104
00:08:46,665 --> 00:08:53,372
Volkmann's canals join neighbouring systems sideways, and the whole bone is wrapped in a periosteum.

105
00:08:55,000 --> 00:09:01,772
Cartilage versus bone: matrix, cells, canaliculi, blood supply and covering.

106
00:09:01,772 --> 00:09:08,153
That missing blood supply is why a torn knee cartilage heals so much more slowly than a broken bone.

107
00:09:08,153 --> 00:09:12,879
And one more favourite: frog bone has no Haversian systems.

108
00:09:12,879 --> 00:09:14,372
Those are a feature of mammals.

109
00:09:15,600 --> 00:09:19,443
Blood is a connective tissue whose matrix is a liquid.

110
00:09:19,443 --> 00:09:29,846
Spin it down: about fifty five percent is plasma, mostly water with proteins, sugar, salts and hormones, and forty five percent is formed elements.

111
00:09:29,846 --> 00:09:38,596
Red cells, biconcave discs with no nucleus in mammals, four and a half to five and a half million in each cubic millimetre.

112
00:09:38,596 --> 00:09:42,151
White cells, four to eleven thousand, for defence.

113
00:09:42,151 --> 00:09:46,734
And platelets, one hundred and fifty to four hundred thousand, for clotting.

114
00:09:47,967 --> 00:09:49,805
A counting chamber example.

115
00:09:49,805 --> 00:09:50,747
Watch the units.

116
00:09:50,967 --> 00:10:03,043
The volume over eighty small squares is eighty times one four hundredth of a square millimetre, times zero point one millimetre deep: zero point zero two cubic millimetres.

117
00:10:03,267 --> 00:10:11,459
Five hundred cells in zero point zero two cubic millimetres is twenty five thousand per cubic millimetre of diluted blood.

118
00:10:11,667 --> 00:10:16,773
Undo the dilution, times two hundred: five million per cubic millimetre.

119
00:10:16,773 --> 00:10:19,624
Inside the normal range, so not anaemic.

120
00:10:22,033 --> 00:10:23,219
Pause and think.

121
00:10:23,219 --> 00:10:24,990
Which of these does cartilage lack?

122
00:10:31,867 --> 00:10:33,171
Blood vessels.

123
00:10:33,171 --> 00:10:40,267
Cartilage is avascular, fed by diffusion from the perichondrium, which is why it heals slowly.

124
00:10:40,267 --> 00:10:44,016
It does have lacunae, chondrocytes, and a perichondrium.

